course: dictionary
lesson: 8281
title: Rinse: a quick wash with clean water
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rinse is a word you will meet in many places: at the sink, in the first-aid box and in science. To rinse something means to wash it quickly with clean water, often to remove soap or dirt. «After dinner you rinse a tin» tells you what to do: a quick splash of water, not a long scrub.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. «If paint splashes in your eye, rinse it with clean water and tell an adult.» The words paint splashes and clean water show that rinse means washing out something that should not be there. Try your meaning in the sentence: wash it out with clean water. It still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Rinse can also be a noun, a naming word. A rinse is a quick wash in water. So you can give a dish a quick rinse, and you can rinse a dish. The ending changes too: rinses, rinsed and rinsing all come from the same word.
fact: To rinse something means to wash it quickly with clean water.
fact: Rinse often removes soap or dirt.
fact: As a noun, a rinse is a quick wash in water.
fact: «Rinse an astro burn well under cool clean running water for about 10 minutes, and tell an adult.» Rinsing a burn helps keep it cool and clean.
fact: «Brush twice a day, then spit but do not rinse, so the fluoride stays on the teeth.» Rinsing the mouth too much can wash the fluoride away.
exercise: «If paint splashes in your eye, rinse it with clean water and tell an adult.» What does rinse mean here? | Rub it hard with a dry cloth | *Wash it quickly with clean water | Cover it with a bandage and wait | Shut your eyes and forget it
exercise: In «Give the dish a quick rinse», what kind of word is rinse? | *A noun, a naming word for a quick wash | An adjective, a describing word | A verb, a doing word | A full stop
exercise: «Chemical spray in the eyes is rinsed out with clean running water for several minutes, and a grown-up is told.» What should you do first if a spray gets in your eyes? | Close them and do nothing | *Rinse them with clean running water and tell a grown-up | Rub them hard | Wait until bedtime
test: Why do you rinse fruit and vegetables before eating them? | To change their colour | *To wash off dirt | To make them heavier | To make them colder
test: Which sentence uses rinse as a noun? | You will rinsed the cup | *Give the bottle a quick rinse | Rinses are good for cleaning | Rinse the bottle now
exam: Why do scientists rinse equipment between tests? | To make it dry faster | To make it smell nice | *So that the results are fair | To keep it warm
